    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Поэтому я не буду давить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Therefore I will not pressure you.' The little negative word sits right in front of the verb for 'will be', just like 'not' in English.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Пусть всё остаётся как было.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let everything remain as it was.' The question word for 'how' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'was'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, но я...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, but I...' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'yes' with 'I'. Then Vika responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мы обязательно будем жить вместе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will definitely live together.' Vika links 'we' directly with 'surely' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А сейчас спокойной ночи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And now good night.' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'night'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До завтра, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Until tomorrow, Max.' Max links 'until' directly with 'tomorrow' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "До завтра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Until tomorrow.' Vika combines 'until' with 'tomorrow' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Переехал жить в подъезд.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Moved to live in the hallway.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'to move' with 'entrance'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi, Max.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'hi' with 'Max'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что тебе надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you need?' Russian builds this impersonally with the dative ('to me' or 'to you') and 'it is necessary'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Макс, возвращайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Max, come back.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Max' paired with the verb for 'return'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Нам тебя не хватает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We miss you.' Kostya links 'us' directly with 'you' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Мы поделим квартиру на троих.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will divide the apartment by three.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'we' with 'three'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я постараюсь приехать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will try to come.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to try', which directs the action back onto the subject. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
